  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Serried porecrust (Fomitopsis serialis) is a small, thin bracket fungus characterized by its densely packed pores and rusty-brown coloration. This wood-decay fungus is commonly found on dead hardwoods and conifers throughout New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Pore density",
      "description": "Very fine, tightly crowded pores (6-8 per mm) that appear almost like a fine velvet or felt surface",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Color and texture",
      "description": "Rusty-brown to ochre-colored upper surface with a thin, brittle fruiting body",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Size and form",
      "description": "Small, thin brackets or crusts, usually less than 5cm wide, often appearing as shelves or crust-like growths",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Serried porecrust grows on dead or dying hardwoods and softwoods, particularly in moist, shaded forest environments throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its small size and delicate appearance, Fomitopsis serialis is an important wood decomposer that plays a crucial ecological role in breaking down dead woody material and recycling nutrients in forest ecosystems.",
